  "question": {
    "text": "The characteristic distance at which quantum gravitational effects are significant, the Planck length, can be determined from a suitable combination of the fundamental physical constants $\\mathrm{G}, \\mathrm{h}$ and $\\mathrm{c}$. Which of the following correctly gives the Planck length?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "k2OE8",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{G}^2 \\mathrm{hc}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "DS7kV",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Gh}}{\\mathrm{c}^3}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "wvkyd",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{G}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\mathrm{~h}^2 \\mathrm{c}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "5X6If",
      "text": "\n$G\\left(h^2 \\mathrm{c}^3\\right.$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Plank length is a unit of length,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&l_p=1.616229 \\times 10^{-35} \\mathrm{~m} \\\\\n&l_p=\\sqrt{\\frac{h G}{c^3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
